※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4565

※ A `&mut` is the only way to the place it names while it lives, and the name
※ is a second one.  Reading through the reference says the same thing and says
※ it once, which is the rule about one meaning and one spelling applied to a
※ place: `r⌖` is what this line is for.
※
※ A `&` lends nothing away that a read would disturb -- it shares, and the name
※ is another sharer -- so a name lent that way stays readable.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let n: mut u8 = 1u8
    let r: &mut u8 = &mut n
    r⌖ ← 2u8
    if n ≠ 2u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
